nnals-of-probability/volume-53/issue-3/Exact-phase-transitions-for-stochastic-block-models-and-reconstruction-on/10.1214/24-AOP1723.full},\n\nnumber = {3},\n\ndoi = {10.1214/24-aop1723}\n\n}--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nExact Phase Transitions for Stochastic Block Models and Reconstruction on Trees 10.1145/3564246.3585155 - Minsky DTIC (https://dtic.minsky.ai/index/10.1145%2F3564246.3585155)\nciteturn3search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.4 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; # Exact Phase Transitions for Stochastic Block Models and Reconstruction on Trees ...   * Elchanan Mossel  * Youngtak Sohn\n# Exact Phase Transitions for Stochastic Block Models and Reconstruction on Trees\n\n## Document Details\n\nDocument Type\n    Pub Defense Publication\n\nPublication Date\n    Jun 02, 2023\n\nSource ID\n    10.1145/3564246.3585155\n\n## Entities\n\n### People\n\n  * Allan Sly\n  * Elchanan Mossel\n  * Youngtak Sohn\n\n### Organizations\n\n  * Massachusetts Institute of Technology\n  * National Science Foundation\n  * Office of Naval Research\n  * Princeton University\n  * Simons Foundation\n\nStatic crawler-friendly document view--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nExact phase transitions for stochastic block models and reconstruction on trees - MaRDI portal (https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/wiki/Exact_phase_transitions_for_stochastic_block_models_and_reconstruction_on_trees)\nciteturn3search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.3 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Authors Elchanan Mossel, Allan Sly, Youngtak Sohn ...   * A computational transition for detecting correlated stochastic block models by low-degree polynomials ... title=Exact_phase_transitions_for_stochastic_block_models_and_reconstruction_on_trees&oldid=76193370\"\n# Exact phase transitions for stochastic block models and reconstruction on trees\n\nFrom MaRDI portal\n\nJump to:navigation, search\n\nDOI10.1214/24-AOP1723MaRDI QIDQ6971346zbMATH OpenFDO\n\nAuthors Elchanan Mossel, Allan Sly, Youngtak Sohn\n\nPublication date 16 June 2025\n\nPublished in The Annals of Probability (Search for Journal in Brave)\n\nFull work available at URL https://doi.org/10.1214/24-AOP1723\n\nzbMATH Keywords\n\nbroadcast processGalton-Watson treesreconstruction problemstochastic block models\n\nMathematics Subject Classification ID\n\nTrees (05C05) Random graphs (graph-theoretic aspects) (05C80) 【23†Branching processes (Galton-Watson, birth-and-death, etc.) --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nExact Phase Transitions for Stochastic Block Models and Reconstruction on Trees (https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.03362)\nciteturn3academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.8 years ago; Title: Exact Phase Transitions for Stochastic Block Models and Reconstruction on TreesAuthors: Elchanan Mossel, Allan Sly, Youngtak Sohn\nTitle: Exact Phase Transitions for Stochastic Block Models and Reconstruction on Trees\nAuthors: Elchanan Mossel, Allan Sly, Youngtak Sohn\nDate: Tue Dec  6 23:11:03 2022\n\nIn this paper we continue to rigorously establish the predictions in ground breaking work in statistical physics by Decelle, Krzakala, Moore, Zdeborov\\'a (2011) regarding the block model, in particular in the case of $q=3$ and $q=4$ communities. We prove that for $q=3$ and $q=4$ there is no computational-statistical gap if the average degree is above some constant by showing it is information theoretically impossible to detect below the Kesten-Stigum bound. The proof is based on showing that for the broadcast process on Galton-Watson trees, reconstruction is impossible for $q=3$ and $q=4$ if the average degree is sufficiently large. This improves on the result of Sly (2009), who proved similar results for regular trees for $q=3$. Our analysis of the critical case $q=4$ provides a detailed picture showing that the tightness of the Kesten-Stigum bound in the antiferromagnetic case depends on the average degree of the tree. We also prove that for $q\\geq 5$, the Kestin-Stigum bound is not sharp. Our results prove conjectures of Decelle, Krzakala, Moore, Zdeborov\\'a (2011), Moore (2017), Abbe and Sandon (2018) and Ricci-Tersenghi, Semerjian, and Zdeborov{\\'a} (2019). Our proofs are based on a new general coupling of the tree and graph processes and on a refined analysis of the broadcast process on the tree.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nISSN 0091-1798 (print) (https://www.imstat.org/publications/aop/aop_53_3/aop_53_3.pdf)\nciteturn3search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.4 years ago; Exact phase transitions for stochastic block models and reconstruction on treesELCHANAN MOSSEL, ALLAN SLY AND YOUNGTAK SOHN  967\nISSN 0091-1798 (print)  \nISSN 2168-894X (online)\n\nTHE ANNALS  \nof  \nPROBABILITY\n\nAn Official Journal of the  \nInstitute of Mathematical Statistics\n\nArticles\n\nBoundary touching probability and nested-path exponent for nonsimple CLE  \nMORRIS ANG, XIN SUN, PU YU AND ZIJIE ZHUANG  797\n\nThe three-dimensional stochastic Zakharov system  \nSEBASTIAN HERR, MICHAEL RÖCKNER, MARTIN SPITZ AND DENG ZHANG  848\n\nA stochastic analysis of subcritical Euclidean fermionic field theories  \nFRANCESCO C. DE VECCI, LUCA FRESTA AND MASSIMILIANO GUBINELLI  906\n\nExact phase transitions for stochastic block models and reconstruction on trees  \nELCHANAN MOSSEL, ALLAN SLY AND YOUNGTAK SOHN  967\n\nUniversality of directed polymers in the intermediate disorder regime  \nJULIAN RANSFORD  1019\n\nRecurrence and transience of multidimensional elephant random walks . . . . . . SHUO QIN  1049\n\nFluctuations and correlations in weakly asymmetric simple exclusion on a ring subject to  \nan atypical current . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . BENOIT DAGALLIER  1079\n\nScaling limits of planar maps under the Smith embedding  \nFEDERICO BERTACCO, EWAIN GWYNNE AND SCOTT SHEFFIELD  1138\n\nVol. 53, No. 3—May 2025--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEXACT PHASE TRANSITIONS FOR STOCHASTIC BLOCK MODELS AND RECONSTRUCTION ON TREES - Princeton University (https://collaborate.princeton.edu/en/publications/exact-phase-transitions-for-stochastic-block-models-and-reconstru-2/)\nciteturn3search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.8 years ago; Crawled: last month; MOSSEL, ELCHANAN, SLY, ALLAN & SOHN, YOUNGTAK 2025, 'EXACT PHASE TRANSITIONS FOR STOCHASTIC BLOCK MODELS AND RECONSTRUCTION ON TREES', Annals of Probability, vol. 53, no. 3, pp.\nkeywords = \"Stochastic block models, broadcast process, reconstruction problem on trees\",\n\nauthor = \"ELCHANAN MOSSEL and ALLAN SLY and YOUNGTAK SOHN\",\n\nnote = \"Publisher Copyright: {\\textcopyright} (2025) (Institute of Mathematical Statistics) All rights reserved.\",\n\nyear = \"2025\",\n\ndoi = \"10.1214/24-AOP1723\",\n\nlanguage = \"English (US)\",\n\nvolume = \"53\",\n\npages = \"967--1018\",\n\njournal = \"Annals of Probability\",\n\nissn = \"0091-1798\",\n\npublisher = \"Institute of Mathematical Statistics\",\n\nnumber = \"3\",\n\n}\n\nMOSSEL, ELCHANAN, SLY, ALLAN & SOHN, YOUNGTAK 2025, 'EXACT PHASE TRANSITIONS FOR STOCHASTIC BLOCK MODELS AND RECONSTRUCTION ON TREES', Annals of Probability, vol. 53, no. 3, pp. 967-1018. https://doi.org/10.1214/24-AOP1723\n\nEXACT PHASE TRANSITIONS FOR STOCHASTIC BLOCK MODELS AND RECONSTRUCTION ON TREES. / MOSSEL, ELCHANAN; SLY, ALLAN; SOHN, YOUNGTAK.\nIn: Annals of Probability, Vol. 53, No. 3, 2025, p. 967-1018.\n\nResearch output: Contribution to journal › Article › peer-review\n\nTY - JOUR\n\nT1 - EXACT PHASE TRANSITIONS FOR STOCHASTIC BLOCK MODELS AND RECONSTRUCTION ON TREES\n\nAU - MOSSEL, ELCHANAN\n\nAU - SLY, ALLAN\n\nAU - SOHN, YOUNGTAK\n\nN1 - Publisher Copyright: © (2025) (Institute of Mathematical Statistics) All rights reserved.\n\nPY - 2025\n\nY1 - 2025\n\nN2 - In this paper we continue to rigorously establish the predictions in ground breaking work in statistical physics by Decelle, Krzakala, Moore, Zdeborová (Phys. Rev. E 84 (2011) 066106) regarding the block model, in particular, in the case of q = 3 and q = 4 communities. We prove that, for q = 3 and q = 4, there is no computational-statistical gap if the average degree is above some constant by showing it is information theoretically impossible to detect below the Kesten–Stigum bound. The proof is based on showing that, for the broadcast process on Galton– Watson trees, reconstruction is impossible for q = 3 and q = 4 if the average degree is sufficiently large. This improves on the result of Sly (Ann. Probab. 39 (2011) 1365–1406), who proved similar results for regular trees for q = 3. Our analysis of the critical case q = 4 provides a detailed picture showing that the tightness of the Kesten–Stigum bound in the antiferromagnetic case depends on the average degree of the tree. Our results prove conjectures of Decelle, Krzakala, Moore, Zdeborová (Phys. Rev. E 84 (2011) 066106), Moore (Bull. Eur. Assoc. Theor. Comput. Sci. EATCS 121 (2017) 26–61), Abbe and Sandon (Comm. Pure Appl. Math. 71 (2018) 1334–1406) and Ricci-Tersenghi, Semerjian, and Zdeborová (Phys. Rev. E 99 (2019) 042109). Our proofs are based on a new general coupling of the tree and graph processes and on a refined analysis of the broadcast process on the tree.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nJournal of Statistical Physics (2024) 191:148 (https://eldorado.tu-dortmund.de/bitstreams/c550c502-a5e4-43f2-b25b-f75b8136acc1/download)\nciteturn3search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Mossel, E., Sly, A., Sohn, Y.: Exact phase transitions for stochastic block models and reconstruction on\n14. Decelle, A., Krzakala, F., Moore, C., Zdeborová, L.: Asymptotic analysis of the stochastic block model\nfor modular networks and its algorithmic applications. Phys. Rev. E 84, 066106 (2011)\n15. Erd˝os, P., Rényi, A.: O